### Summary of Key Insights
The retrieved evidence demonstrates that there is no scientific evidence or documented medical case reports linking the use of face masks to *Staphylococcus* infections. Medical experts and dermatologists have explicitly stated that face masks do not cause staph infections, and research suggests that wearing a mask may actually help prevent the nasal carriage of *Staphylococcus aureus*. While wearing a mask creates a warm, moist microenvironment that can allow bacteria to multiply on the skin and proper mask hygiene is recommended to prevent skin irritation, experts agree that masks are not a cause of bacterial infections like staph. Because the evidence is clear and conclusive, no further investigation is required.

### Conclusion
Based on the findings of the fact-check, the claim that wearing face masks can cause infections from bacteria such as staphylococcus is explicitly contradicted by medical consensus and scientific data. Dermatologists and infectious disease experts agree that there is no association between mask use and staph infections, and there are no documented cases of such infections being directly caused by masks. Therefore, the final decision is `refuted`.

### Verdict: REFUTED

### Justification
There is no scientific evidence or documented medical case reports linking the use of face masks to bacterial infections like staph. According to medical experts and dermatologists interviewed by [USA Today](https://www.usatoday.com/story/news/factcheck/2020/09/18/fact-check-staph-infections-common-not-caused-face-masks/5692064002/), face masks do not cause staph infections, and they are no more likely to cause skin abrasions than other clothing. While wearing a mask can create a warm, moist environment that allows bacteria to thrive on the skin as noted by [Clicks](https://clicks.co.za/beauty-blog/article-view/Is-your-mask-a-breeding-ground-for-bacteria-we-chat-to-an-expert), practicing proper mask hygiene can easily prevent bacterial issues according to [Health Desk](https://health-desk.org/articles/is-there-any-evidence-for-a-relationship-between-staph-infections-and-using-masks-to-prevent-the-spread-of-covid-19). Furthermore, a study published in [PLOS ONE](https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371/journal.pone.0165713) suggests that wearing a mask may actually help prevent, rather than cause, the nasal carriage of *Staphylococcus aureus* bacteria, which is already commonly found on healthy human skin and nasal passages as detailed by [Stanford Medicine](https://med.stanford.edu/news/all-news/2013/12/staph-can-lurk-deep-within-nose-study-finds.html). Additionally, a clinical trial on [PubMed](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/24472436/) indicates that certain masks, like N95 respirators, are significantly protective against bacterial colonization compared to medical masks.